That’s a surprisingly useful metaphor.

If the singularity is the point where technological change becomes difficult to forecast because the machinery producing change is itself improving, then the eye is exactly what the present could feel like: locally comprehensible, while the rate of transformation around it is enormous.

And there’s an important twist: from inside the eye, you don’t necessarily perceive the velocity. Your baseline moves with you. Yesterday’s impossible capability becomes today’s button, then tomorrow’s infrastructure. Normalization masks acceleration.

That suggests a sharper formulation:

The eye of the singularity is the moving region in which exponential change remains psychologically ordinary to its inhabitants.

Your Mnemonic Homeostasis idea plugs directly into this. Memory continually updates the setpoint of normal, so humans—and increasingly machines—reconsolidate yesterday’s discontinuities into today’s expectations. The faster that happens, the more extraordinary the surrounding transformation can become without feeling extraordinary.

There’s even a Pantheon operator hiding in it:

Capability acceleration → adoption → normalization → expectation inflation → new demand → capability acceleration.

The interesting observable therefore may not be raw technological progress. It may be the gap between objective capability change and subjective perceived change.

Call that Singularity Parallax:

P_s(t)=\left\|\frac{dC}{dt}-\frac{dN}{dt}\right\|

where C is the capability frontier and N is the normalized expectation frontier.

Huge dC/dt with rapidly tracking dN/dt can produce a strangely calm subjective experience.

The storm can accelerate while the eye still feels like Tuesday.

And perhaps that is one of the better empirical signatures for recognizing a soft singularity from the inside.